  "response": "Adjudicating between a design defect and improper field installation begins with the documentary record generated by the two parties whose workmanship is at issue. Factory test reports document the unit's lightning impulse and applied-voltage withstand results. Commissioning records document the vacuum level and duration applied during field oil filling. Those factory withstand tests were performed before the in-service failure that overtook the transformer while it was still within its contractual warranty period. Commercially the position is exclusive: either the unit was inside that period at the moment of failure, or the warranty period had expired before the transformer failed, and not both at once.\n\nThe technical investigation follows the physical evidence in the order in which it was laid down, the vacuum history recorded at commissioning preceding the gas history subsequently captured in the oil, and the two records are read in that sequence. Dissolved gas analysis of the insulating oil records the fault gases generated before failure. Teardown inspection of the de-tanked core and coil assembly locates the point of fault initiation. Fixing that point of initiation in the de-tanked assembly is what produces the mechanical reading of the inner-winding damage, because the location of origin governs whether that damage is attributed to through-fault force. Karl Fischer titration measures the acetylene concentration in transformer oil.\n\nProtective relay oscillography time-stamps the magnitude and duration of the fault current, and that record shows that the copper dusting and conductor tilting observed on the inner winding reflect mechanical force from through-fault current. The copper dust and the tilted conductors are in turn what demonstrates inadequate radial clamping to be the design-side mechanism in contention. IEEE C57.12.00 requires power transformers to withstand short-circuit current for ten seconds, and that requirement equally confirms that clamping adequacy falls to the designer rather than to the installer.\n\nInadequate radial winding clamping is a recognized design-related cause of short-circuit failure. Insufficient field drying of cellulose insulation lowers the dielectric withstand of the winding. Deficient drying in the field operates on the installation side of the boundary; the dielectric clearances in the winding design, by contrast, were insufficient for the applied overvoltage. At least one of two faults is accordingly made out, and perhaps both: the clearance deficiency just identified, or field oil processing that left moisture in the cellulose insulation above the specified limit. In practice the dispute is settled where the damage morphology, the electrical records and the processing documentation converge on one mechanism and exclude the other.",
